#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045464 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/review #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1007 "Linear Algebra" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Linear Algebra course builds on students' prior experience with matrices and determinants to develop their understanding of crucial linear algebra concepts. These include systems of equations, vector spaces, linear transformations, eigenvectors and eigenvalues, diagonalization, matrix functions, inner products, orthogonal projections, quadratic forms, and singular value decomposition. Real-world applications such as least-squares problems, Markov chains, and regression will also be explored.\nThis course is designed to give students a deep understanding of linear algebra, equipping them with the knowledge and skills necessary for advanced study in mathematics, computer science, physics, and engineering." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1009 "After briefly looking at some essential set theory, logic, and vector geometry, students explore matrices in-depth. They will become fluent in Gaussian elimination, using it to solve systems of equations and compute inverse matrices.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces. They will explore vectors in N-dimensional space, linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, nullity, and the rank-nullity theorem. They also generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces.\nThis course develops students' understanding of linear transformations, including images, kernels, linear maps between different vector spaces, and change-of-coordinates matrices.\nStudents will learn how to find and solve the characteristic equation of a matrix and fluently compute eigenvectors. They will study matrix diagonalization and its properties, apply the rotational-scaling theorem, and explore matrix functions. They will also cover generalized eigenvectors and Jordan canonical decomposition.\nThis course covers various aspects of orthogonality in vector spaces, including orthogonal sets, complements, and orthogonal matrices. In addition, students will learn about orthogonal projections and the Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process.\nStudents will explore quadratic forms, including positive-definite and negative-definite quadratic forms, and the diagonalization of symmetric matrices. They will also classify quadratic curves and learn how to reduce a quadratic curve to its principal axes.\nFinally, this course explores singular value decomposition and other applications of linear algebra, such as linear least-squares problems, Markov chains, and regression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1012 55 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1013 "LAL" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "f4899d31-ceac-5f10-b529-994cde2dd912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1007 "Methods of Proof" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1008 "This course introduces students to proof-writing essentials, bridging the gap between pure problem-solving and constructing formal mathematical arguments. Students will formalize their understanding of logic, sets, and functions while delving into new concepts such as congruence, relations, and cardinality.\nThis course serves as ideal preparation for students wishing to pursue undergraduate studies in formal mathematical disciplines, including Discrete Mathematics, Abstract Algebra, and Real Analysis.\nThe prerequisite for Methods of Proof is single-variable calculus, which would be satisfied by completion of either Calculus II, AP Calculus BC, or Mathematical Foundations III." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1009 "Students begin by mastering the basics of logic and logical operations, including statements, predicates, connectives, logical laws, logical equivalence, conditional and biconditional statements, quantifiers, inference, and formal and informal language. By solidifying their grasp of these logical fundamentals, students will have a secure underpinning that enables them to embark on their journey into proof-writing confidently.\nA robust knowledge of set theory is another cornerstone of mathematical proof-writing. Students will extend and formalize their understanding of sets and learn to translate between logical and set operations. By the end of the course, students will appreciate how set theory provides a comprehensive toolkit for proving mathematical results.\nAfter mastering these fundamentals, students will explore various proof-writing techniques, including direct proof, proof by cases, proof by contrapositive, proof by contradiction, disproof, and mathematical induction. The course emphasizes proving concepts related to parity, divisibility, congruence, sequences, functions, set identities, and relations. Students will have multiple opportunities to test and refine their proof construction and comprehension skills throughout the course and in varied contexts\nThis challenging yet engaging course helps students with strong mathematical foundations build a solid base for further progression into advanced undergraduate courses." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1012 76 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1013 "MOP" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "53322b4c-7036-5cde-89d2-8e430f49ef2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428276 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1031 "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1033 153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1034 0.964428309771597 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048146 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "832923c0-f0e3-599f-af85-37cb7dae2658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428342 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1031 "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1033 1776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1420f0fa-c9b8-5cba-8bba-e14cbf53c041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1031 "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1033 1775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2eee4710-3f26-5186-948b-a819cfaf4083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "beff9032-09df-5ed1-b8f3-e06b2ccf3918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119600 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119600 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4x-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x+y-z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119600 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1x-1y+3z &=-1 \\\\ 4x+0y-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x+1y-1z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(-4+6)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =2+2+12 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-1)+(-8-3)-(4-0) \\\\  & =-1-11-4 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-16}{16}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119602 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119602 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2,$ find the value of $k.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=-1 \\\\ kx+3y-2z &=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119602 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-2)-2(2k+6)-(-k-9) \\\\  & =4-4k-12+k+9 \\\\  & =1-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x = \\dfrac{D_x}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(6-2)-2(2+6)-(-1-9) \\\\  & =-4-16+10 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10}{1-3k} & =2 \\\\ -10 & =2-6k \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119667 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119667 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-6z &=8 \\\\ -6x+4y+2z &=3 \\\\ 5x-5y-15z &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119667 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y+(-6)z &=8 \\\\ (-6)x+4y+2z &=3 \\\\ 5x+(-5)y+(-15)z &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n![](images/00/00a69fe1d48b41e376971f3669ae51668733d36dfda18c0f9cc736d5d071a441.png)\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n![](images/14/14e850c9af7c0b2fdd1e36299667b2cbe91c84e8bb90030b729ee23b296ad984.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119791 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119852 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1045 "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119998 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8734274c-15b6-589a-a77f-6fd025ab063a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y-z &=1 \\\\ 2x-3z &=-2 \\\\ -x+y-2z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $42,$ then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cf1a77a-780e-5431-8f91-cd363218bcc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120440 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y+(-1)z &=1 \\\\ 2x+0y+(-3)z &=-2 \\\\ (-1)x+1y+(-2)z &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0+3)-5(4+18)-(-2-0) \\\\  & =3-110+2 \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{-105}{42}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+z=4 \\\\ y-4z=-6 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{y}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b69dc5d3-fe00-5843-9b79-6c2a6e202607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120441 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1x+(-3)y+1z=4 \\\\ 0x+1y+(-4)z=-6 \\\\ 3x+1y+1z=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+z &=5 \\\\ x+y-2z &=-3 \\\\ -5x+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $7,$ then $y=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f118e080-b2e1-5b06-9da5-b10b39fd54f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-3)y+1z &=5 \\\\ 1x+1y+(-2)z &=-3 \\\\ -5x+0y+4z &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{y},$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the second column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(-12-2)-5(4-10)+(-1-15) \\\\  & =-70+30-16 \\\\  & =-56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{-56}{7}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120648 #edb/ref 1061 16705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "620dc976-5ed3-54d8-a37a-7483259c65bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120649 #edb/ref 1061 16707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c34a23fc-50c9-5fb1-b89d-0a53b98720ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120651 #edb/ref 1061 16711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd4aa514-64cb-5bde-8bf4-8e5364299dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120706 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120706 #edb/ref 1061 17835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120706 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9ce92e1-dc54-5375-b685-ec0fb5099a5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120706 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120745 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120745 #edb/ref 1061 23061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120745 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5e91e09-6b2c-5a23-a95e-c5b4f8576a52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120745 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121026 #edb/ref 1064 "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/39/39c4a39573975acc249a4402799f40ef8d72666d4da03d6c4ed6cc299bc7fc52.svg). \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n![](images/dc/dcda1374b4582e7978133bbf3cea0375b40095d353573f826a3bac7f212dfc03.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121026 #edb/ref 1065 3079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f245bf99-3b45-5a27-9aca-aaee391628ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121026 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121047 #edb/ref 1064 "To demonstrate using Cramer's rule on a $3 \\times 3$ system, consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+z=9 \\\\ 2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+1z=9 \\\\ 0x+2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+1y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(12-3)-2(0+24)+(0+16) \\\\  & =36-48+16 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $D_x,$ $D_y,$ and $D_z.$\n\n- To find $D_x,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *first* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(12-3)-2(42-0)+(7-0) \\\\  & =81-84+7 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_y,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *second* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(42)-9(0+24)+(0+56) \\\\  & =168-216+56 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}|+9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-7)-2(0+56)+9(0+16) \\\\  & =-28-112+144 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩ & =⟨\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{4},\\frac{8}{4},\\frac{4}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121047 #edb/ref 1065 3241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121047 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9d916943-4be4-554c-a4f5-51aef55e8254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121047 #edb/ref 1067 "A Demonstration of Cramer's Rule on a 3x3 Linear System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269463 #edb/ref 1080 "k=9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "178aa29c-c731-5575-b789-a031a7794737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269733 #edb/ref 1080 "k=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269733 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269733 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32d6fe63-36e7-505d-b5ca-f8377d01d86a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269885 #edb/ref 1080 "k=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44193ff7-7933-5662-b23c-3e02bf36d32b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270549 #edb/ref 1080 "k=11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270549 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270549 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9261b35a-1876-58e0-8b49-edd341555666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270628 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270628 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270628 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9a5e86f2-180e-58a4-a5c7-b73059c1110a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271885 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2a02390-118a-5af6-b56f-a5456ae3d683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y-3z &=2 \\\\ 4x-5y+kz &=1 \\\\ -2x+10y-5z &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $160-70k,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "254e5808-cdaa-58f1-bc6f-06ea580b1b72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272008 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|=160-70k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & k \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(25-10k)-5(-5+2k)-3(10-10) \\\\  & =50-20k+25-10k+0 \\\\  & =75-30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(75-30k) & =160-70k \\\\ 150-60k & =160-70k \\\\ 10k & =10 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327225 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327225 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327225 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21a06992-2a94-5a3a-a20b-d5286ce30bcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327230 #edb/ref 1080 "k=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327230 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327230 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2241ec88-5356-5893-b69f-c9e4fd87c09c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327329 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2cd9079a-4963-509d-a3da-d27366b782b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327414 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327414 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327414 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "377efdb7-7cee-5099-8cae-51cdbbe6616b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327449 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327449 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327449 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3aea6d24-0a56-5888-a8e3-109a62413c53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327522 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "439acbed-ae94-5186-8b18-2a5683b17f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327615 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327615 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327615 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4e3d3044-4f96-5fc4-9219-3888cf7c6c74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327665 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327665 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592